1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
How do you follow one payment through the whole system?

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:06,912
A merchant calls: my customer's payment has been spinning for a minute.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:14,208
The payment crossed the payment page, three modules, the card vault, an adapter and a card network, on four machines.

4
00:00:14,258 --> 00:00:18,624
Without one number to follow, someone searches four log files and guesses.

5
00:00:18,674 --> 00:00:22,752
And the log lines that would help are the ones most likely to hold a card number.

6
00:00:22,802 --> 00:00:28,224
In a hospital, your wristband number is on every chart, in every department.

7
00:00:28,274 --> 00:00:31,392
Anyone can follow your whole stay by that one number.

8
00:00:31,442 --> 00:00:38,832
Here, every request gets one trace, and its trace id travels on every call, across both units.

9
00:00:38,882 --> 00:00:45,792
Every response names that id in a header called X-Request-Id, even when the answer is a refusal.

10
00:00:45,842 --> 00:00:47,856
So a caller can quote it to support.

11
00:00:47,906 --> 00:00:53,112
Every log line carries the same id, so a warning links straight to its trace.

12
00:00:53,162 --> 00:00:56,856
And log masking cleans every line the running app writes.

13
00:00:56,906 --> 00:01:03,576
Card numbers keep only their first six and last four digits, tokens their last four, and secrets are hidden.

14
00:01:03,626 --> 00:01:08,544
Each unit sends all of this to its own Grafana, and the card zone runs its own.

15
00:01:08,594 --> 00:01:12,456
A dashboard shows how payments end, and which are still unknown.

16
00:01:12,506 --> 00:01:18,072
And alert rules, written as code, send an email when payments stay unknown too long.

17
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:19,944
Now read it in the code.

18
00:01:19,994 --> 00:01:25,776
One small filter runs before security, so even a refused request carries its id.

19
00:01:25,826 --> 00:01:29,976
It takes the current trace, and writes its id into the response.

20
00:01:30,026 --> 00:01:35,400
The masking keeps the first six digits, stars out the middle, and keeps the last four.

21
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:41,280
And any run of thirteen to nineteen digits is treated as a card number, wherever it appears.

22
00:01:41,330 --> 00:01:46,584
Like a hospital wristband: one number on every chart, and no secrets written on it.

23
00:01:46,634 --> 00:01:49,104
Next: what the merchant gets each day.
